<!--
    GH-1276: "8051: while loop condition not simplified."
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/1276

    The 8051 spec models the SUBB borrow / CY flag as the high (sign) bit of a
    byte, so a boolean condition `b` is lowered into a sign test by shifting `b`
    into bit 7 and signed-comparing against 0:  `(b << 7) s< 0`.  This is exactly
    `b != 0`, but upstream has no rule for the boolean-into-sign-bit form, so the
    shift-and-sign-test survives all the way into the rendered loop condition:

      while ((BANK0_R4 < uVar1) << 7 < '\0') { ... }

    Stage model: S3 "simplification-quiescence" (which algebraic/peephole rules
    fire).  kuna's RuleBoolSignLess (group "analysis", gated by the default-off
    arch flag fold_flag_compare) recognizes `INT_SLESS(INT_LEFT(b,k), 0)` where
    b's NZMask is exactly the single bit that the shift lands in the sign
    position, and rewrites it to `b != 0`, dropping the shift entirely.
    Exposed by `option flagcompare on`.

    Pass 1 (default pipeline) reproduces the bug: the `<< 7 < ` shift-into-sign
    survives (assert #1).
    Pass 2 (after `option flagcompare on`) the shift is gone and the condition is
    a clean boolean comparison: no `<< 7` appears anywhere (assert #2).
-->
